1
00:00:02,344 --> 00:00:04,705
By now, we learned a lot about keeping our

2
00:00:05,074 --> 00:00:08,126
list of function parameters short and

3
00:00:08,126 --> 00:00:11,257
concise and keeping the function body short

4
00:00:11,273 --> 00:00:11,980
and concise.

5
00:00:12,638 --> 00:00:13,618
Now, there is another

6
00:00:14,148 --> 00:00:17,392
aspect of writing clean functions which you

7
00:00:17,392 --> 00:00:18,307
should keep in mind,

8
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:20,764
and that is that you should try

9
00:00:21,294 --> 00:00:23,109
to keep your functions pure.

10
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:25,550
Now, what does this mean though? What is a

11
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:26,851
pure function?

12
00:00:27,412 --> 00:00:30,746
A pure function is simply a function which

13
00:00:31,243 --> 00:00:34,273
for the same inputs generates the same

14
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:35,171
output.

15
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:39,162
So for the same input parameter values, you

16
00:00:39,258 --> 00:00:41,647
always get back the same output.

17
00:00:42,625 --> 00:00:45,350
And this here would be a simple example,

18
00:00:45,590 --> 00:00:47,418
this generateId function.

19
00:00:48,556 --> 00:00:51,746
It takes a username as a parameter. It then

20
00:00:52,002 --> 00:00:55,128
combines that with a hardcoded string and

21
00:00:55,224 --> 00:00:58,158
then it returns this ID string which was

22
00:00:58,350 --> 00:00:58,879
generated.

23
00:00:59,873 --> 00:01:01,556
Now, as you can see, if you have a look at

24
00:01:01,556 --> 00:01:05,099
this code, for the same username value like

25
00:01:05,307 --> 00:01:08,770
Max, we will always get back exactly the

26
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:10,405
same return value.

27
00:01:11,014 --> 00:01:14,637
This is a function which is 100% predictable

28
00:01:15,022 --> 00:01:16,657
and which therefore is pure.

29
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:20,248
Now, here's the same function again, now,

30
00:01:20,536 --> 00:01:23,758
however, defined as an impure function.

31
00:01:24,319 --> 00:01:27,012
This function, unlike the first one, does

32
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,866
not always yield the same result for the

33
00:01:29,866 --> 00:01:33,233
same input, because we do generate a random

34
00:01:33,297 --> 00:01:35,943
number which we convert to a string which we

35
00:01:35,943 --> 00:01:38,396
then combine with the entered input value.

36
00:01:39,407 --> 00:01:42,325
So for the same input value, we will always

37
00:01:42,421 --> 00:01:44,458
get a different output value here.

38
00:01:44,987 --> 00:01:47,889
Hence, this function is not pure.

39
00:01:48,691 --> 00:01:52,139
Now, it's not necessarily bad to have impure

40
00:01:52,139 --> 00:01:54,913
functions in your code and in your program

41
00:01:55,426 --> 00:01:57,318
because sometimes you just need something

42
00:01:57,351 --> 00:01:59,404
like this. Sometimes you might need to

43
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:02,564
generate a random ID and return that. So

44
00:02:02,564 --> 00:02:05,644
it's not the case that impure functions are

45
00:02:05,756 --> 00:02:08,820
always bad, but they are definitely less

46
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,788
predictable than pure functions. And hence,

47
00:02:11,868 --> 00:02:15,173
you should only go for impure functions if

48
00:02:15,221 --> 00:02:17,323
you really need that behavior.

49
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:20,371
And that's especially true if we don't just

50
00:02:20,371 --> 00:02:23,868
consider the generation of a random value as

51
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:25,825
part of your function, but if we

52
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:28,199
specifically have a look at another concept,

53
00:02:28,552 --> 00:02:29,385
side effects.

54
00:02:30,315 --> 00:02:33,344
Pure functions shouldn't have side effects,

55
00:02:34,065 --> 00:02:36,549
which again does not mean that you are not

56
00:02:36,549 --> 00:02:38,040
allowed to have side effects at all,

57
00:02:38,633 --> 00:02:40,732
but a pure function wouldn't have a side

58
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:42,912
effect and therefore you might wanna

59
00:02:42,912 --> 00:02:45,669
minimize the number of functions that do

60
00:02:45,765 --> 00:02:46,630
have side effects.

61
00:02:47,528 --> 00:02:50,204
Especially when following a functional

62
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:52,240
programming approach, if you're really

63
00:02:52,256 --> 00:02:54,724
trying to strictly follow such an approach,

64
00:02:55,317 --> 00:02:58,427
it is quite common and expected that the

65
00:02:58,443 --> 00:03:01,092
vast majority of your functions which you

66
00:03:01,092 --> 00:03:03,035
compose and combine together

67
00:03:03,565 --> 00:03:06,101
are side-effect-free and pure.

68
00:03:07,257 --> 00:03:09,730
And then you would only have a couple of

69
00:03:09,922 --> 00:03:12,732
places in your code where you do have side

70
00:03:12,732 --> 00:03:13,181
effects.

71
00:03:13,792 --> 00:03:15,847
Now, of course, you don't have to follow the

72
00:03:15,847 --> 00:03:17,661
functional programming approach.

73
00:03:18,271 --> 00:03:20,390
You could have an object-oriented approach

74
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:23,216
or kind of a mixed approach, an imperative

75
00:03:23,328 --> 00:03:25,496
way of writing code, and therefore you don't

76
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:27,953
always have to be that strict But in

77
00:03:28,049 --> 00:03:30,475
general, as you will also see over the next

78
00:03:30,475 --> 00:03:32,886
minutes, side effects are definitely

79
00:03:32,886 --> 00:03:35,344
something you should be aware of when you

80
00:03:35,441 --> 00:03:36,469
write clean code.

81
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:40,229
But for that, let's first of all clarify

82
00:03:40,679 --> 00:03:42,688
what actually is a side effect.

83
00:03:43,555 --> 00:03:45,596
Well, here's a simple example snippet.

84
00:03:46,223 --> 00:03:49,308
This createUser function does what the name

85
00:03:49,356 --> 00:03:49,886
implies.

86
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:52,522
It creates a user by

87
00:03:53,052 --> 00:03:55,200
instantiating a user object by using the

88
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:55,793
constructor,

89
00:03:56,402 --> 00:03:58,181
and then it returns the user.

90
00:03:58,759 --> 00:04:02,141
So like this, it would be a pure function.

91
00:04:02,574 --> 00:04:05,299
It will always give us the same kind of user

92
00:04:05,315 --> 00:04:07,752
object whenever we run this function.

93
00:04:08,425 --> 00:04:11,294
However, let's now say we have this line of

94
00:04:11,391 --> 00:04:12,721
code in the function as well,

95
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:15,863
this startSession line of code.

96
00:04:17,114 --> 00:04:20,752
Now this function which we call here is very

97
00:04:20,849 --> 00:04:24,215
likely to change the overall state

98
00:04:24,776 --> 00:04:27,469
of our program and that's what we call a

99
00:04:27,517 --> 00:04:28,271
side effect.

100
00:04:28,944 --> 00:04:32,134
It's an operation which does not just act on

101
00:04:32,182 --> 00:04:34,619
the function inputs and change the function

102
00:04:34,796 --> 00:04:35,100
output

103
00:04:35,757 --> 00:04:38,547
but which instead changes the overall system

104
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:39,957
or program state.

105
00:04:41,064 --> 00:04:43,821
For example, startSession could change some

106
00:04:44,109 --> 00:04:47,011
variable outside of this createUser

107
00:04:47,059 --> 00:04:47,476
function.

108
00:04:48,181 --> 00:04:50,442
And when you have such a side effect, a

109
00:04:50,442 --> 00:04:53,665
function is also not considered to be pure

110
00:04:54,034 --> 00:04:56,536
because even if it does always return the

111
00:04:56,536 --> 00:04:59,390
same value for the same input, if it does

112
00:04:59,583 --> 00:05:03,175
something else in addition to the work it

113
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:06,142
does on the inputs, then it's also not

114
00:05:06,142 --> 00:05:07,521
considered to be pure.

115
00:05:08,339 --> 00:05:11,257
So a pure function is only pure if it always

116
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:13,503
gives us the same output for the same input

117
00:05:13,919 --> 00:05:16,421
and it doesn't have any side effects.

118
00:05:17,223 --> 00:05:19,562
But what's the problem with such side

119
00:05:19,562 --> 00:05:20,075
effects?

120
00:05:20,732 --> 00:05:23,200
Again, you won't be able to always avoid

121
00:05:23,296 --> 00:05:25,764
them. Indeed, depending on the kind of

122
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:28,280
program you're writing, you might need a lot

123
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,020
of side effects because there are a lot of

124
00:05:31,084 --> 00:05:33,936
potential side effects. Starting sessions,

125
00:05:34,145 --> 00:05:37,061
sending HTTP requests, even logging

126
00:05:37,109 --> 00:05:39,369
something to the console or showing some

127
00:05:39,417 --> 00:05:42,061
message to the user would all be a side

128
00:05:42,061 --> 00:05:44,918
effect because it's a change to the state of

129
00:05:44,934 --> 00:05:48,178
your system and of your program, right? I

130
00:05:48,178 --> 00:05:49,832
mean, if you show something to the user,

131
00:05:50,089 --> 00:05:52,128
that's definitely a different state your

132
00:05:52,256 --> 00:05:55,018
program is in than a second ago when you

133
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:55,934
didn't show a message.

134
00:05:57,250 --> 00:06:00,141
So these changes are everywhere and side

135
00:06:00,141 --> 00:06:01,409
effects are everywhere.

136
00:06:02,052 --> 00:06:05,247
But side effects do become a problem if they

137
00:06:05,408 --> 00:06:06,564
are unexpected.

138
00:06:07,415 --> 00:06:09,968
They're not automatically bad, we need them.

139
00:06:10,562 --> 00:06:13,501
But if your side effect is unexpected, then

140
00:06:13,999 --> 00:06:14,737
it is a problem.

141
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:17,884
For example, in this createUser function,

142
00:06:18,254 --> 00:06:21,096
you could kind of expect a side effect.

143
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:24,419
Maybe createUser implies that the user is

144
00:06:24,676 --> 00:06:27,550
also stored in a database and that would

145
00:06:27,647 --> 00:06:28,883
also be a side effect.

146
00:06:29,927 --> 00:06:32,544
Starting a session could also be a kind of

147
00:06:32,544 --> 00:06:35,691
expected side effect. So in this example, it

148
00:06:35,691 --> 00:06:37,891
might not be a bad side effect at all.

149
00:06:38,742 --> 00:06:40,857
But consider this example here.

150
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:44,528
It's again the generateId function, but now

151
00:06:44,736 --> 00:06:47,669
it's not just generating and returning an

152
00:06:47,702 --> 00:06:51,789
ID, it's also assigning that generated ID to

153
00:06:51,885 --> 00:06:55,636
some global state, to some global variable

154
00:06:55,716 --> 00:06:57,976
which we have in this program. This is a

155
00:06:57,992 --> 00:07:00,989
side effect because it's not just work we do

156
00:07:01,005 --> 00:07:03,409
on the input and output of this function, it

157
00:07:03,409 --> 00:07:06,022
is work we do on some other variable in our

158
00:07:06,214 --> 00:07:06,663
program.

159
00:07:07,850 --> 00:07:10,497
Now maybe we need to keep track of that last

160
00:07:10,497 --> 00:07:12,758
assigned ID. Maybe that is some internal

161
00:07:12,871 --> 00:07:13,464
work we do,

162
00:07:14,090 --> 00:07:16,865
but maybe it's also not something we expect

163
00:07:17,041 --> 00:07:20,185
when we call a function named generateId.

164
00:07:20,971 --> 00:07:23,233
Maybe a simple change in the name to

165
00:07:23,233 --> 00:07:27,147
generateUnusedId would suffice here though

166
00:07:27,484 --> 00:07:30,163
to make it clear that this function will

167
00:07:30,291 --> 00:07:33,178
need to check whether an ID has been used or

168
00:07:33,178 --> 00:07:36,002
not and that therefore it might also

169
00:07:36,066 --> 00:07:38,231
interact with the global system state.

170
00:07:38,809 --> 00:07:40,957
So that could be one possible fix here

171
00:07:41,342 --> 00:07:43,603
instead of just keeping the name generateId

172
00:07:43,603 --> 00:07:43,972
like this

173
00:07:45,127 --> 00:07:48,062
because that is the strategy for avoiding

174
00:07:48,559 --> 00:07:51,429
unexpected side effects. As I said, and I

175
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:53,690
really wanna emphasize this, side effects

176
00:07:53,770 --> 00:07:56,320
are not necessarily bad but your naming

177
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:59,960
matters. Side effects do become bad if they

178
00:08:00,265 --> 00:08:01,308
are unexpected

179
00:08:02,366 --> 00:08:04,787
and therefore the name of a function should

180
00:08:04,787 --> 00:08:06,215
signal or imply

181
00:08:06,776 --> 00:08:09,037
that a side effect is likely to occur.

182
00:08:09,647 --> 00:08:11,944
Otherwise, it's considered a bad side effect

183
00:08:12,072 --> 00:08:15,011
and therefore not clean code because clean

184
00:08:15,044 --> 00:08:18,465
code doesn't do unexpected stuff. Clean code

185
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:21,388
is, well, clean, easy to read and easy to

186
00:08:21,388 --> 00:08:24,182
understand. And if something happens which

187
00:08:24,182 --> 00:08:26,672
you didn't expect, you clearly did not

188
00:08:26,688 --> 00:08:28,936
understand the code, hence the code wasn't

189
00:08:28,936 --> 00:08:29,306
clean.

190
00:08:30,141 --> 00:08:31,875
So have a look at these example names here,

191
00:08:31,972 --> 00:08:32,598
for example.

192
00:08:33,128 --> 00:08:36,198
If you see functions named like this, Where

193
00:08:36,552 --> 00:08:38,276
would you expect a side effect?

194
00:08:40,178 --> 00:08:43,110
In saveUser, we do expect one, I would

195
00:08:43,174 --> 00:08:46,508
argue, because saveUser implies that this

196
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:48,971
user is probably going to get saved to a

197
00:08:48,971 --> 00:08:52,256
database and saving data to a database

198
00:08:52,498 --> 00:08:55,216
definitely is a side effect because it does

199
00:08:55,248 --> 00:08:57,820
manipulate the overall system and program

200
00:08:57,820 --> 00:08:58,222
state.

201
00:08:59,170 --> 00:09:01,211
After all, we write a new entry to a

202
00:09:01,211 --> 00:09:04,716
database, we change files. This is a crystal

203
00:09:04,831 --> 00:09:05,680
clear side effect and we do expect

204
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:10,081
such a side effect for a function named

205
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:10,802
saveUser.

206
00:09:11,972 --> 00:09:13,718
On the other hand, if we had a function

207
00:09:13,782 --> 00:09:15,080
named isValid,

208
00:09:15,753 --> 00:09:18,477
then we do not expect any side effects

209
00:09:18,477 --> 00:09:22,226
there. We do expect that this function maybe

210
00:09:22,306 --> 00:09:24,838
answers the question whether some input or

211
00:09:24,966 --> 00:09:27,770
some email address is valid. It would be

212
00:09:27,850 --> 00:09:31,022
very much unexpected that this function then

213
00:09:31,551 --> 00:09:34,067
sends a message, writes to the file system,

214
00:09:34,403 --> 00:09:37,207
shows a message to the user, or changes any

215
00:09:37,385 --> 00:09:38,459
other program state.

216
00:09:40,310 --> 00:09:41,442
Now, what about showMessage?

217
00:09:42,299 --> 00:09:45,760
showMessage per definition will have a side

218
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:49,286
effect. If we show a message to the user, no

219
00:09:49,302 --> 00:09:51,777
matter if that's the terminal or console

220
00:09:52,456 --> 00:09:56,030
or if that is an overlay on a website, if we

221
00:09:56,030 --> 00:09:58,246
show a message to the user, we are

222
00:09:58,521 --> 00:10:00,683
definitely changing the program state

223
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:03,844
because the state will be a different one if

224
00:10:03,844 --> 00:10:06,749
we have an overlay on the page than it was a

225
00:10:06,749 --> 00:10:09,189
second ago when the overlay was not there.

226
00:10:10,104 --> 00:10:13,073
So here, because of that name, a side effect

227
00:10:13,073 --> 00:10:13,939
would be expected.

228
00:10:15,015 --> 00:10:16,315
Now, what about createUser?

229
00:10:17,101 --> 00:10:19,493
Here, I would say you could go either way.

230
00:10:19,894 --> 00:10:22,398
You could say that you don't expect a side

231
00:10:22,398 --> 00:10:26,185
effect because a function named createUser

232
00:10:26,570 --> 00:10:29,588
will probably do just that, create a new

233
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:30,021
user.

234
00:10:30,888 --> 00:10:33,039
On the other hand, create is a very

235
00:10:33,777 --> 00:10:37,052
wide word with a lot of interpretation you

236
00:10:37,068 --> 00:10:37,790
can put into it.

237
00:10:38,512 --> 00:10:41,096
Create could mean that saving that user to

238
00:10:41,096 --> 00:10:44,034
the database is part of the creation process

239
00:10:44,772 --> 00:10:47,966
or that validation of the inputs and showing

240
00:10:47,999 --> 00:10:50,535
an error message is part of the creation

241
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:51,016
process.

242
00:10:52,172 --> 00:10:54,628
So here it really comes down to the overall

243
00:10:54,724 --> 00:10:57,597
context of the application in which you use

244
00:10:57,629 --> 00:11:00,054
this function. A side effect is not

245
00:11:00,054 --> 00:11:02,832
necessarily expected, I would say, but it's

246
00:11:02,912 --> 00:11:04,742
also not totally unexpected.

247
00:11:05,401 --> 00:11:07,745
And over the last lectures, we indeed had a

248
00:11:07,745 --> 00:11:10,282
function named createUser which did have

249
00:11:10,395 --> 00:11:11,743
various side effects.

250
00:11:13,044 --> 00:11:15,983
And therefore, long story short, the idea is

251
00:11:16,015 --> 00:11:19,033
simple. It is okay to have impure functions

252
00:11:19,290 --> 00:11:20,960
and it is okay to have side effects.

253
00:11:21,554 --> 00:11:24,172
But whenever a function is impure, typically

254
00:11:24,204 --> 00:11:26,676
because it does have a side effect, that

255
00:11:26,676 --> 00:11:28,040
should at least be expected.

256
00:11:29,002 --> 00:11:31,104
Your functions should not have unexpected

257
00:11:31,104 --> 00:11:31,810
side effects

258
00:11:32,371 --> 00:11:34,472
and if you have a side effect or if you need

259
00:11:34,472 --> 00:11:36,943
a side effect, you should therefore either

260
00:11:37,183 --> 00:11:39,237
choose a function name which implies that,

261
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:41,338
then it's absolutely fine,

262
00:11:42,076 --> 00:11:44,129
or you should move that side effect

263
00:11:44,257 --> 00:11:47,065
operation into a different function where it

264
00:11:47,065 --> 00:11:48,075
again is expected.

265
00:11:48,974 --> 00:11:51,444
This is important to ensure that you have

266
00:11:51,476 --> 00:11:54,289
clean code and clean functions. Because it's

267
00:11:54,289 --> 00:11:57,006
definitely not clean code if your functions

268
00:11:57,120 --> 00:11:59,077
do something which is not expected.
